  "text": "Three left, one breath each. Log compaction keeps at least the latest value for each key and clears out older ones, instead of expiring by age. The topic becomes a snapshot of current state, like each user's latest profile, to rebuild state from. It runs alongside time-based retention. A batch join can wait for every row; a stream join can't, because neither side ends. Stream to stream, it buffers each side's recent events in a time window, matches across them, and lets the watermark expire old state. Stream to table, each event looks up a table kept current, often a compacted topic. And a dead-letter queue catches messages a consumer can't process: malformed, off-schema, or failing again and again. The main stream keeps flowing instead of stalling on one poison message; the bad ones wait to be inspected, fixed and replayed. A batch pipeline sets bad rows or whole tasks aside the same way, so a few don't stop everything, and none vanish. Every one of these streams lands somewhere, and how it's stored decides what reading it costs. Next: data warehousing and storage.",
